ent places\" gives no cause that can be checked against a map or data. Correction: Canberra is higher above sea level and further from the sea''s mild influence, so winter nights are colder and frosts are more common. Why: the correction restores accurate use of climate.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w15-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise climate.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use climate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify coast and explain why it matters.","I can practise inland independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cause needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Places differ because of location. Nearness to the equator, being on the coast or inland and height above sea level all change temperature and rainfall. People''s choices, resources and history also make places different. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose…","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Places differ because of location. Nearness to the equator, being on the coast or inland and height above sea level all change temperature and rainfall. People''s choices, resources and history also make places different.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about climate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Darwin is hot all year because it is closer to the equator. Hobart is cooler because it is further south and close to the Southern Ocean. Coastal places often have milder temperatures than inland places at the same latitude.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy might Canberra, which is inland and higher above sea level, have colder winters than Sydney on the coast?\n\nFEEDBACK\nCanberra is higher above sea level and further from the sea''s mild influence, so winter nights are colder and frosts are more common.","explain":"Places differ because of location. Nearness to the equator, being on the coast or inland and height above sea level all change temperature and rainfall. People''s choices, resources and history also make places different.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying \"they are different because they are different places\" gives no cause that can be checked against a map or data. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Latitude and temperature.","caption":"Distance from the equator matters.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Darwin</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">near the equator, hot all year</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Hobart</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">further south, cooler</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: Darwin is hot all year because it is closer to the equator. Hobart is cooler because it is further south and cl Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: Darwin is hot all year because it is closer to the equator. Hobart is cooler because it is further south and cl Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about climate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Darwin is hot all year because it is closer to the equator. Hobart is cooler because it is further south and close to the Southern Ocean. Coastal places often have milder temperatures than inland places at the same latitude. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Coastal and inland differences.","caption":"Height above sea level matters too.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Coast</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">milder temperatures</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Inland</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">colder winters, more frost</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a compa